## Setup

Install [Homebrew](https://brew.sh), then run in the repository:

```sh
brew install mise gh                              # or another installer from mise.jdx.dev
echo 'eval "$(mise activate zsh)"' >> ~/.zshrc    # optional: the tools on PATH in your shell
mise trust && mise install                        # Rust, cargo-nextest, cargo-deny, cargo-shear
mise exec -- rustup component add rust-analyzer   # editor support for the pinned Rust
git config core.hooksPath .githooks
gh extension install github/gh-stack              # optional: stacked PRs
```

mise installs the tools at the versions in `mise.toml`, with the checksums in `mise.lock`. It
installs Rust through rustup, at the version in `rust-toolchain.toml`. rustfmt and Clippy come
with that Rust. CI installs the same versions from the same files. The git hook runs the checks
before each push. `gh stack` is optional: it helps to open and update stacked PRs.

Without mise, rustup still reads `rust-toolchain.toml`. Install cargo-nextest, cargo-deny and
cargo-shear at the versions in `mise.toml` yourself, for example with `cargo binstall`.

## Commands

```sh
scripts/check                       # every quality gate: format, lint, dependencies, build, test
mise exec -- cargo run
mise exec -- cargo nextest run      # the tests, without the doc examples
mise exec -- cargo test --doc       # the doc examples
mise exec -- cargo fmt
mise exec -- cargo build --release
```

`scripts/check` is the single list of gates. The git hook and CI run it too.
`.github/workflows/scheduled.yml` runs two more checks each week that never block a merge: the
RustSec security advisories, and the property tests with random inputs.

